# Bot signals for the bot detection (O722; server/README.md "Bot detection events and scores (O722)", docs/BOT_DETECTION.md): what the
# engine sees of a player's walking and targeting that Lua cannot, as counts and a few times a script polls. Detection and reports only:
# nothing here changes how a player walks, attacks or is treated, and nothing is saved.
#   * Player::getChaosBotSignals() holds a ChaosBotSignals::Signals (src/chaosbotsignals.h, pure, tested by server/tools/chaosbotsignals_test.cpp):
#     key steps, path commands (a click on the map or the minimap: one command of N steps) and the time from a monster coming into the player's
#     view to the player's FIRST attack on it (a reaction time, at most 30 s, a ring of 64 waiting creatures and 32 reaction times).
#   * Hooks, one line each: Game::playerMove (a key step), Game::playerAutoWalk (a path command, listDir.size() steps),
#     Player::onCreatureMove (a monster that was out of the player's view and is in it now), Game::playerSetAttackedCreature (a new target
#     that is a monster).
#   * Lua player:getBotSignals(): {keys, paths, pathSteps, reactions = {{creature id, ms}, ...}} since the last call and resets, nil when there
#     is nothing. The capability scripts detect: type(Player.getBotSignals) == "function". Without the patch botdet.lua has no reaction or
#     path data and the other four heuristics carry the total.
# Applies after 0001-0092 (0028 kite walk, 0066 attack at once, 0091 and 0092 give the context lines). The context of the two hunks of
# player.cpp and game.cpp that 0028 / 0066 do not show (Creature::onCreatureMove's call line, the blank lines) is the stock TFS 1.4.2 text; the
# base source tree is not in the repository, so the patch was written from those patches and from the stock text and NOT applied to a tree:
# run patch -p1 --fuzz=0 --dry-run first (server/tools/botsignals_patch_test.py does it with SRC_TREE=<tree with 0001-0092>), then rebuild.
